-- title:
--   K-rationality of the weight-one twist of Tate(mathsf q^q)
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime and $M'\ge 1$ with $q\nmid M'$, and let $\ell_g$ be a prime with $\ell_g\equiv 11\pmod{12}$ and $\ell_g\mid M'$. Let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero, $\xi\in L$ a primitive $(q\ell_g)$-th root of unity, and assume some ring homomorphism $\iota\colon L\to\mathbb C$ sends $\xi$ to $e^{2\pi i/(q\ell_g)}$. Let $H_1\le(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times$ be the intersection of the kernels of reduction to $(\mathbb Z/q)^\times$ and to $(\mathbb Z/\ell_g)^\times$, and let $K\subset L(\!(\mathsf q)\!)$ be the $L$-subfield generated by the coefficientwise image under $\mathbb Q\to L$ of the $q$-expansion function field `xHFunctionField` of level $(q^2M',H_1)$. Write $\zeta$ for the unit of $L$ attached to $\xi$. Then there is a Weierstrass variable change $C=(u,r,s,t)$ over $L(\!(\mathsf q)\!)$ with $r=-1/12$, $s=-1/2$, $t=1/24$ (constant Laurent series) normalised by $u\cdot(2x+1/6)=2y+x$, where $(x,y)=$ `tateToricPoint L q` $(\zeta^{q})$, such that: all five coefficients $a_1,a_2,a_3,a_4,a_6$ of $C\bullet$ `tateBase L q` (the Tate curve with $\mathsf q$ substituted by $\mathsf q^{q}$) lie in $K$; for all nonzero $v,w\in(\mathbb Z/q)^2$ the $xP$ and $yP$ coordinates of `cuspData L q` $(\zeta^{\ell_g})\,v\,w$ transformed by $C$ lie in $K$ (these two coordinates involve only $v$); and for every $c\in L^\times$ with $c^{\ell_g}=1$, $c\ne 1$, the $C$-transform of the level-$P$ datum whose $P$- and $Q$-coordinates are both `tateToricPoint L q c` has $xP,yP\in K$.
--
--   This is the rationality statement for the weight-one (Deligne–Rapoport/Katz–Mazur normalised) twist of the Tate curve with parameter $\mathsf q^{q}$, asserting that the twisted curve together with its level-$q$ cusp points and its toric $\ell_g$-torsion points is defined over the modular function field of level $H_1$ inside $L(\!(\mathsf q)\!)$. It is used in the construction of the diamond-operator level automorphisms at the cusp, in the passage to the raw rigidified Tate datum, and in the computation of the $j$-invariant of the Tate curve over this field.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_FullLevel_Diamond_exists_variableChange_weightOne_tateBase_mem_laurentBaseChange_and_cuspData_mem_of_exists_ringHom_pinGamma1.lean

